                         ). This carbon partitioning not only stabilizes the RA but also improves the deformability of martensite  [45] . Another notable heterogeneous element distribution in Q&P steel is the severe co-segregation of carbon and manganese at the RA/FM interface. The significantly higher diffusion coefficient of carbon compared to manganese leads to a more pronounced carbon segregation at the phase boundary under the current alloying and heat treatment conditions. This phenomenon of carbon and manganese co-segregation has been reported to increase the interface stability, resulting in the enhancement of critical stress for martensite transformation  [23,46] . The microscopic strengths of different constituents, as revealed by the nanohardness measurements shown in  Fig. 3 , demonstrate great agreement with the alloying elements distributions. The higher nanohardness of FM compared to CM in Q&T steel, and the lower nanohardness of all constituents in Q&P steel compared to Q&T steel, are statistically clarified. Moreover, RA, typically considered a compliant zone among the hard martensitic matrix  [47,48] , exhibits a comparable average nanohardness to the FM and CM due to its stabilization through carbon partitioning  [23,49] .
